## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design end-to-end Azure OpenAI-powered data-governance tooling that handles multi-tenant isolation, secure KB grounding, and robust testing.

## Key Concepts
- Data lineage ingestion and normalization across Synapse/Data Factory.
- Versioned KB in Azure Blob and Cognitive Search integration.
- Per-tenant isolation with Key Vault-backed Cosmos DB storage.
- Prompt engineering with fallback chains and confidence scoring.
- Data redaction and auditability.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for gap-filling using KB
async function fillLineageGap(partialLineage, kbClient) {
  if (partialLineage.isComplete()) return partialLineage;
  const kb = await kbClient.query(partialLineage.pattern);
  return partialLineage.enrich(kb);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure the accuracy of enrichment?
- How would you handle cycles or conflicting KB results?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests hands-on ability to weave Azure AI, policy governance, and regulatory constraints into a live assistant that scales per tenant and remains auditable.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI behind API Management
- Grounding with Cognitive Search over versioned KB in Blob
- Per-tenant policy gating via Key Vault keys
- PII redaction with Text Analytics
- Audit logs in Data Lake
- Fallback to sanitized checklist on gate/confidence failure

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: gate checks before OpenAI call
function shouldFallback(prompt, budget, policy) {
  if (policy.violationDetected) return true;
  if (estimateCost(prompt) > budget) return true;
  if (promptContainsPII(prompt)) return true;
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test per-tenant isolation and key management across regional deployments?
- How would you validate redaction quality without leaking schemas or patterns to vendors?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates practical API boundary design, basic rate limiting, and graceful degradation in a beginner-friendly Azure setup.

## Key Concepts
- API Management quotas and rate limits by user
- Per-user spend cap and simple state store
- Fallback to rule-based replies on failure
- Observability for quotas and failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal mock of request flow with quota check and fallback
async function handle(req) {
  const user = req.userId;
  const quota = getUserQuota(user);
  if (quota.remaining <= 0) return cannedReply();
  try {
    const res = await callOpenAI(req.payload);
    debitQuota(user, req.payload.length);
    return res;
  } catch {
    return cannedReply();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota expiry and fallback paths?
- How would you simulate OpenAI downtime in a local test environment?
